  "summary": "Recent preprints and a Current Biology paper test selection, drift and environment as drivers of trait and lineage diversification across birds, lizards, insects, mammals and plants.",
  "synthesis": "The items share a macroevolutionary question: how do selection, drift and ecological context shape phenotypic and lineage diversity? Sexual dichromatism in iridescent birds is explained by both Darwinian and Wallacean processes, while leaf-footed bug dimorphism is labile and often arises already dimorphic in new traits. In island kingfishers, color evolution accelerates as genomic diversity declines, implicating drift. Bark anoles show convergent signal evolution decoupled from genomic species boundaries, with gene flow shaping speciation outcomes. Environmental associations appear in Bergmann's rule attenuation by thermal similarity among bird congeners and in larger auditory bullae with aridity in Sminthopsinae. Mimosa shows a continuum of diversification rates with radiations tied to abiotic niche lability. The dominant thread is that lability and demographic context, not a single mechanism, structure trait evolution.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-19"
}
